{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>She shall not go out as the going out of the slaves.</b> Like slaves who go out by tooth and eye, but rather she serves for six years, or until the Jubilee, or until she develops signs. According to its plain sense, it appears fitting to give a reason for all these matters of freedom in the case of the Hebrew slave, whether male or female, or the Canaanite slave — for every Canaanite upon whom the light of the Torah has not shone has no purpose other than to make silver and gold, to heap up grain like the sand of the sea, and to amass wealth; and for this he makes himself like a pack-bearing donkey, and behold he is a slave bored through to his master's acquisitions all his days, forever. And that gathered wealth has no purpose other than to feed his teeth and his eyes. His teeth and body — how so? For through his wealth he obtains all kinds of foods that enter through the mouth and the grinding teeth, and as long as his grinding teeth are strong and healthy, he imagines that their power now is as it was then to grind any food; the man does not rest until such time as they have ceased eating and the grinders have failed because they are few (Ecclesiastes 12:3). But before this he forever stands under the yoke of this slavery, and when his days grow long and no food any longer pleases his palate, then he rests and then he is a free man — for why should he toil for the wind, as Barzillai the Gileadite said (II Samuel 19:36): ״I am eighty years old today; can I still taste anything?״ His eyes — how so? Sometimes, and for the most part, you find a man who hoards more than all the camps beyond the measure of his eating and his other needs, and it is only to feed his eyes with it, as it says (Proverbs 27:20): ״And a man's eyes are never sated,״ and it is written: ״And what advantage is there to its owners except the seeing of his eyes?״ (Ecclesiastes 5:10). And as long as his eyes are in his head he is never sated and has no freedom at all; but when he grows old and his eyes grow dim from seeing and those who look through the windows are darkened, then from the toil of his soul he is sated — for for whom is he toiling, if his eyes are dim and the grinders have ceased? And as long as both are in their strength, he leaps from species to species and sweeps from this to that, as explained in our work עוֹלְלוֹת אֶפְרַיִם in the passage about Hormiz bar Lilita, who used to sweep from this to that. Therefore the Torah gave freedom to the Canaanite slave through the loss of tooth and eye, measure for measure — for this is precisely the freedom he himself would have chosen from the toil of his soul, for he too never ceases pursuing after vanity except through the loss of tooth and eye." "But not such is the portion of Jacob, for every man of Israel in whom the Torah of HaShem dwells does not seek illusory acquisitions except only as a measure to bring a remission of hunger to his household, so that his limbs may be strong and healthy to engage in the Torah of HaShem that was given on the seventh — on the day of Sabbath, a day free from labor. For on that very day he is free from labor in order to engage in the Torah that was given over the span of fifty days, drawn from the Jubilee. And when he has so much in his hand that he can engage in Torah without distraction, then he is a free man, casting from his shoulders the burden of illusory acquisitions and placing on his neck the yoke of Torah, alluded to in the six and in the Jubilee. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said (Avot 6:2): there is no free man except one who engages in Torah, etc. — for he does not wait for his freedom until old age, as is the way of the nations who go out by tooth and eye, but even in his youth he is a free man, when he has in hand enough for his sustenance so that he can engage in the Torah that was given after six days and at the Jubilee. Therefore, measure for measure, the Torah gave freedom to the Hebrew slave through six and Jubilee. And in addition to this, a Hebrew maidservant goes out by signs, to teach that her principal existence is for the perpetuation of the species, to bear children from her; therefore, immediately when she develops signs and is fit to bear children, he is obligated to free her in order to pair her with a husband, for for this she was created." "And there is yet another allusion in this entire passage, that the number seventh is distinguished from the six in three places: at the beginning of the passage, ״and in the seventh he shall go out free״ (Exodus 21:2); at the end of all the laws between man and his fellow it is written, ״but in the seventh you shall let it rest and lie fallow, that the poor of your people may eat״ (Exodus 23:11); and in the middle of the entire passage — as the Yalkut concludes in this passage — there are in the entire passage sixty commandments up to the passage of the Sabbatical year, and in the passage of the Sabbatical year ten commandments, making the total seventy. And it is certain that this number is not an empty thing. And what seems closest to me to say regarding this is that everyone who does wrong and violence with money has covered his eyes from seeing, for not at his death does he take everything, and he cannot use all that is his except for seventy years — the appointed span for all the living. And when the seventh year approaches he goes out free, as it is written (Psalms 88:6): ״among the dead, free,״ ״but in the seventh you shall let it rest and lie fallow, that the poor of your people may eat״ — for most of his use of them is ״with you to the grave״ (Job 5:26), according to the numerical value of ״כֶּלַח״ — seventy. But the last ten years he should spend building for his eternal home, and he should remember that at his death everything is ownerless, and he has no advantage from his toil. And if this matter were constantly before his eyes as a reminder, he would not do wrong to pervert a man in his cause, for that which he has is not his: ״an acquisition that is not his — what is it to him?״ (piyyut for the second day of Shavuot). Therefore the Torah alluded to freedom in the seventh year in these three places in this passage, all of which is built on the foundation of the laws between man and his fellow, and this is the secret of the number sixty commandments, and the seventh passage — that is, the Sabbatical year — ten commandments, and those who understand will comprehend."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:22:29.922459681Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}